WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.290
From ambitious, mind-bending sci-fi movies
to murder mysteries and war epics, Christopher

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.960
Nolan has put a unique stamp on Hollywood
during his two-decade career as a director

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.580
— and proved that ambitious, original ideas
can make just as much box office bank as comic

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.460
book superheroes.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.960
"If you're good at something never do it for
free."

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.740
With a level of critical and commercial success
that most filmmakers never come close to,

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.940
Nolan's only real competition in the industry
is himself.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.200
But how do his movies stack up against each
other?

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.280
Here's our ranking, starting with number ten.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.420
Following

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.460
Even Christopher Nolan's worst movie is pretty
darn good.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.460
But more than anything, it stands as a testament
to his early promise as a filmmaker.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.820
Clocking in at just over an hour, this super-short
movie revolves around a man whose hobby is

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.950
following strangers — which it turns out
is pretty weird when you're doing it in real

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:45.540
life and not on Twitter.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:51.060
"When it stopped being random, that's when
it started to go wrong."

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.100
With a narrative that jumps around between
the future and present, it's no surprise that

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.280
critics see Following as a harbinger of the
great work Nolan would produce in years to

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:58.780
come.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.180
Insomnia

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.560
Nolan's breakout as a filmmaker came with
Memento in 2000 — but it's the movie he

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.280
made after that one that's next in our reverse
ranking.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:12.660
This suspenseful crime thriller is a remake
of the 1997 Norwegian film of the same name,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.620
and Nolan used his Alaskan setting to spectacular
effect.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.040
And in addition to the kind of intricate plot
that would become a trademark of his work,

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:24.229
the director also proved that he could wrangle
a talented cast of A-listers; Insomnia is

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:28.400
led by Al Pacino and Robin Williams, who delivered
one of his most chilling performances.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.160
The Prestige

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.520
Smart, mysterious, and featuring a totally
underrated cameo by David Bowie, this period

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.220
piece pulls off one of the best twists in
modern cinema.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.880
The story follows a pair of dueling illusionists,
played by Hugh Jackman and Christian Bale,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.220
as they battle to rule the magic scene in
late 1800s London.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:52.600
Hey!

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.240
Where'd he go?

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.800
We won't spoil the big reveal, but let's just
say that this movie has a lot up its sleeve,

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.920
making it a memorable and ambitious entry
into Nolan's catalogue.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.760
The Dark Knight Rises

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.520
The final entry in Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy
is a fantastic film.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.820
It's just not as good as the first two, which
is why it's here on the bottom half of the

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:10.320
list.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.440
Nolan brought his series to a rousing end
by turning the entire city of Gotham into

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.820
a war zone, with imposing bad guy Bane, played
by Tom Hardy, as the villain-in-chief.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.200
Along with Anne Hathaway as Selina Kyle, The
Dark Knight Rises brought a brand of spark

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.760
and wit the franchise had been missing.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:28.540
And even if the formula was arguably stretched
a bit thin by now, the director still managed

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.300
to send his Batman out with a bang.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:31.840
Interstellar

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.080
Nolan walked a tightrope with this sci-fi
flick, combining a space adventure with a

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.060
metaphysical message about the transcendent
power of love.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:39.980
Ambitious!

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.120
But Nolan worked hard to get the science right
and create a stunning blockbuster spectacle,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.560
while also never losing touch with his story's
heart.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.540
At the middle of it all is a mission to save
humanity — and a poignant performance by

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.240
Matthew McConaughey as a hero on the verge
of a life-changing sacrifice.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:55.540
Memento

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.700
We're into the top five, with the movie that
first put Nolan on the map — and a textbook

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.820
example of what makes him such a great filmmaker.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.580
Starring Guy Pearce as the world's most unreliable
narrator, it forces the audience to piece

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.950
together its story one tiny detail at a time,
backwards, using only a bunch of polaroids

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.850
and tattooed clues on the skin of its protagonist…
who hasn't the foggiest idea how his own story

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:17.350
began.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.060
"Look, I'm sorry I don't remember you.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.200
It's nothing personal."

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.900
Fortunately, nobody has amnesia when it comes
to recognizing Memento as Nolan's first cinematic

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:26.520
masterpiece.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:31.600
As both writer and director, he showed Hollywood
he had the chops to take big, original, high-concept

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.120
ideas and turn them into something critics
and audiences alike would rave about.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.020
Batman Begins

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.400
"He's here."

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:38.980
"Who?'

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:41.200
"The bat man!"

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.000
This superhero franchise was in shambles when
Christopher Nolan's pitch for a back-to-basics

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.340
approach put it back on track, and reinvented
the genre in the bargain.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:54.120
Batman Begins nailed the classic character's
origin story and introduced a whole new generation

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.930
of fans to the Dark Knight — and it put
Batman back on top of the box office for the

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:02.100
first time in ages, proving that its director
had the chops to helm blockbusters that were

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.300
so much more than simple popcorn flicks.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:05.680
Inception

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.880
Let's be honest: for most directors, setting
an action-packed heist film inside multiple

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.100
sub-layers of an increasingly complex dreamworld
would have resulted in a mess of epic proportions.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.020
But for Christopher Nolan, that premise made
for a mind-bending masterpiece.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.900
With an all-star cast led by Leonardo DiCaprio,
Joseph Gordon-Levitt, Tom Hardy, and Ellen

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.970
Page, Inception may still be the director's
most remarkable project: a dizzyingly complex

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.360
story and jaw-dropping visual effects extravaganza
in one.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.200
What might be most impressive, though, is
that Nolan is one of few directors who Hollywood

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.240
would dream of trusting with a non-franchise
picture this ambitious.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:41.500
Dunkirk

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.910
Despite the uncountable number of World War
II movies that have come out of Hollywood,

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:49.880
Christopher Nolan still managed to give the
military conflict a fresh spin with this story

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:54.880
about the daring real-life evacuation of Allied
troops from the French city of Dunkirk.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.500
Instead of a big, sweeping battlefield epic,
Nolan got right up close to the action with

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.870
multiple perspectives across three different
timelines — featuring a talented ensemble

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.840
cast that included a few familiar faces from
Gotham City.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.980
Critics were seriously impressed, and this
inspirational film is the director's best

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.500
yet… with one exception.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:13.840
The Dark Knight

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.240
If Nolan's Batman trilogy is the best superhero
franchise ever made, this movie is its crown

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:19.740
jewel.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:24.960
"Hiiiii."

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.670
The late Heath Ledger stole every scene with
a mesmerizing, Oscar-winning performance as

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:33.660
the Joker, and Christian Bale was in full
command of the caped crusader's cowl — not

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.030
to mention his memorable bat-voice.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:39.180
"WHERE ARE THEY?!"

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.780
Critics and audiences alike loved The Dark
Knight, and many people were furious when

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:47.330
it was snubbed in the Best Picture category,
and for good reason: it's not just one of

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:51.640
the best superhero movies of the past decade,
it's one of the best movies, ever, period

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.220
— and that's why it's number one on our
list of Nolan's greatest hits.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:55.880
No clowning.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.220
Thanks for watching!

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:59.290
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:01.600
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

